1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
C has no string type. A string is a run of char objects ending at the first byte of value zero.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The literal "GATE" is five bytes: G, A, T, E, and the zero.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
printf with %s copies bytes until it meets the zero. It has no other way to know the length.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So two numbers describe every string: bytes stored, and bytes before the zero.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
char *s = "GATE" stores a pointer. The five bytes live somewhere else, with static storage duration.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
char t[] = "GATE" stores a copy of the five bytes in t itself, and t is an array.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Writing through s is undefined behaviour. The standard says a program that modifies that array has undefined behaviour.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Three differences follow: t is writable, sizeof(t) is five, and t cannot be reassigned.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
sizeof is answered by the compiler from the declared type. strlen is answered at run time by counting.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
For char a[] = "GATE", sizeof is 5 and strlen is 4. The difference is the terminator.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
sizeof counts bytes that are not there yet; strlen stops at the first zero.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
An array passed to a function is a pointer, so sizeof inside that function reports the pointer.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
char t[10] = "GATE" gives ten bytes: five used, five zero-filled.

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
char c[4] = "GATE" gives four bytes and no zero. c is a char array, not a string.

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Brace initialisation is the other way in, and it adds no terminator of its own.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Only one byte may ever be dropped, and only the terminator, and only when the array is one short.

17
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
An escape sequence is one byte in memory, however many characters it takes to write.

18
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
"\0" has sizeof 2 and strlen 0, because its only content byte is the zero.

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Adjacent literals are joined before anything else, so "ab" "cd" is one literal of five bytes.

20
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Hex and octal escapes are read greedily, so a following digit can be swallowed.

21
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:40,000
strlen returns size_t. The subtraction of two size_t values is unsigned, so it never goes negative.

22
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:48,000
On a 64-bit machine, 3 - 5 is 18446744073709551614. On the 32-bit convention it is 4294967294.

23
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:56,000
So strlen(s) - strlen(t) > 0 is true whenever the two lengths differ at all.

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:04,000
The same unsigned type breaks a downward loop: for (i = strlen(s) - 1; i >= 0; i--) never ends.

25
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The subscript is defined as a sum, so 2[p] and p[2] are the same object.

26
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
In "GATECSIT2017", p[2] is T and p[6] is I, with values 84 and 73.

27
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,000
The argument becomes c + 11 - 1, which is c + 10, pointing at "17".

28
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
strlen of the two remaining characters is 2, and that is the printed answer.

29
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:42,000
A copy loop that tests the source byte and copies it never writes the terminator.

30
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
GATE 2025 Set 2 Q9: a[30] holds "@#Hello World!", and the call copies from a + 2.

31
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Indices 12 and 13 still hold d and !, and the zero at 14 was never touched.

32
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:08,000
printf therefore prints "Hello World!d!". Option D of the paper.

33
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
char names[3][8] is one object of 24 bytes. Each row is eight bytes, used or not.

34
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
char *names[3] is an array of three pointers. sizeof is three pointer widths.

35
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
A row of the grid is a char[8]; an element of the pointer array is a char *.

36
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
This is why main receives argv as char *argv[], and why argv[1] may be reassigned.

37
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Step one: find the object. Is it an array or a pointer? sizeof is decided here.

38
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Step two: lay out the bytes, including the zero, and count what strlen would see.

39
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Step three: check the types of any arithmetic. size_t is unsigned, so a difference is never negative.

40
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Step four: ask whether the terminator was written. If not, the object is not a string.

